---
import PageSection from "../../components/PageSection.astro";
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { copy } from "../../lib/i18n";

const lang = "en";
const text = copy[lang];

const schema = [
  {
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    itemListElement: [
      {
        "@type": "ListItem",
        item: "https://getlilo.app/",
        name: "Lilo Browser",
        position: 1,
      },
      {
        "@type": "ListItem",
        item: "https://getlilo.app/arc-alternative/",
        name: "Arc alternative",
        position: 2,
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://getlilo.app/arc-alternative/#faq",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    inLanguage: "en",
    mainEntity: [],
    url: "https://getlilo.app/arc-alternative/",
  },
];
---

<Layout
  lang={lang}
  title="Lilo Browser: a free Arc alternative for Mac, without Chromium"
  description="Looking for an Arc alternative? Lilo Browser keeps Spaces, the sidebar, split view and Peek, imports your Arc spaces, and runs on WebKit instead of Chromium. Free and open source."
  schema={schema}
  hero={{
    heading: text.arcHeading,
    description: text.arcIntroduction,
    tag: text.arcAlternative,
    link: { href: "#compare", label: text.arcHeroLink },
  }}
  closing={{
    heading: text.arcClosingHeading,
    description: text.arcClosingDescription,
  }}
>
  <PageSection id="familiar">
    <div class="head">
      <span class="tag">What feels familiar</span>
      <h2>The Arc way of browsing, still here.</h2>
      <p>
        The ideas that made Arc different are the ones Lilo is built around.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="feat-list two">
      <div>
        <b>Spaces.</b> Keep work, personal and private browsing apart, each with
        its name, color, emoji and, if you like, its own profile with its own
        sign-ins and extensions.
      </div>
      <div>
        <b>A sidebar.</b> Tabs on the left like Arc, or on the right, top or
        bottom. Pinned tabs, favorites and folders you can nest and lock.
      </div>
      <div>
        <b>Split view.</b> Up to four pages side by side, or a terminal beside
        your work.
      </div>
      <div>
        <b>Peek and the Little window.</b> Links from pinned tabs open over the
        page, and a quick window from any app takes one link or one search, then
        goes to a Space if it turns into something.
      </div>
      <div>
        <b>Command bar.</b> Tabs, bookmarks, history and every action from one
        field, with <kbd>⌘T</kbd>.
      </div>
      <div>
        <b>Tab archive.</b> Unpinned tabs clear themselves into an archive, with
        a delay you set per Space, and come back when you need them.
      </div>
      <div>
        <b>Your shortcuts and link rules.</b> Start from the Arc preset in
        Settings, then remap anything. Rules send links from other apps to the
        Space you pick, like Air Traffic Control.
      </div>
      <div>
        <b>Your extensions.</b> Manifest V3 extensions from the Chrome Web Store
        install with one click and keep updating.
      </div>
    </div>
  </PageSection>

  <PageSection id="compare">
    <div class="head">
      <span class="tag">Side by side</span>
      <h2 id="arc-comparison">Arc and Lilo, compared.</h2>
      <p>Two sidebar browsers for the Mac, built in very different ways.</p>
    </div>
    <div
      class="matrix"
      role="region"
      aria-labelledby="arc-comparison"
      tabindex="0"
    >
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <td></td>
            <th scope="col">Arc</th>
            <th scope="col">Lilo</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Engine</th>
            <td>Chromium</td>
            <td>WebKit, the engine Safari uses</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">New features</th>
            <td class="n">Shuts down December 22, 2026</td>
            <td class="y">Public beta, regular releases</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Price</th>
            <td>Free</td>
            <td>Free</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Source code</th>
            <td class="n">Closed</td>
            <td class="y">Open source, MIT</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Account</th>
            <td>Required</td>
            <td>None</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Spaces and profiles</th>
            <td class="y">Yes</td>
            <td class="y">Yes</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Chrome Web Store extensions</th>
            <td class="y">Yes</td>
            <td class="y">Manifest V3</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Terminal tabs</th>
            <td class="n">No</td>
            <td class="y">Yes</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">AI agents</th>
            <td class="n">No MCP server</td>
            <td class="y">Local MCP server, opt-in</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Sync</th>
            <td class="y">Yes</td>
            <td class="n">No</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Platforms</th>
            <td>Mac, Windows</td>
            <td>Mac only, macOS 26 or later</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </PageSection>

  <PageSection id="move" layout="steps">
    <div class="head" style="margin:0">
      <span class="tag">Moving from Arc</span>
      <h2>Your Arc spaces come with you.</h2>
      <p>
        Lilo reads Arc on your Mac and changes nothing in it.{" "}
        <a href="/moving/">Everything about moving to Lilo</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="claims">
      <div class="claim">
        <span class="ic" style="background:#b39df4">
          1
        </span>
        <div>
          <h3>Spaces, tabs and splits</h3>
          <p>
            Each Arc space becomes a Lilo Space with its icon and color, its
            pinned and open tabs, folders and splits of up to four tabs. Arc
            favorites become bookmarked tabs.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="claim">
        <span class="ic" style="background:#9ac7eb">
          2
        </span>
        <div>
          <h3>History and sign-ins</h3>
          <p>
            Your most recent pages fill the command bar, and your cookies are
            copied so you stay signed in. macOS asks once to let Lilo use Arc's
            "Safe Storage" key.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="claim">
        <span class="ic" style="background:#9bd3b4">
          3
        </span>
        <div>
          <h3>Extensions</h3>
          <p>
            The ones from the Chrome Web Store are installed again from the
            store, up to date. You pick which.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </PageSection>

  <PageSection id="faq" layout="faq">
    <div class="head" style="margin:0">
      <span class="tag">Questions</span>
      <h2>Leaving Arc.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-list">
      <details>
        <summary>Is Arc being discontinued?</summary>
        <p>
          Yes. The Browser Company stopped building new features for Arc in 2025
          to focus on Dia, and
          <a href="https://x.com/arcinternet/status/2113301454597640570">
            announced
          </a>
          that it shuts Arc down on December 22, 2026. Lilo reads your Arc
          spaces straight from your Mac, so import them while Arc is still
          installed.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Is Lilo based on Chromium, like Arc?</summary>
        <p>
          No. Lilo is built on WebKit, the engine that ships with macOS, in an
          app written in Swift. No Chromium is bundled, which keeps it light on
          memory and battery, and it still runs Chrome extensions.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Can I import my Arc spaces?</summary>
        <p>
          Yes. Each space comes in as a Lilo Space with its icon, color, pinned
          and open tabs, folders and splits. Anything in Arc's sidebar that is
          not a tab, a folder or a split is skipped.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Will my Arc keyboard shortcuts work?</summary>
        <p>
          Pick the Arc preset in Settings, General, Shortcuts, then remap
          anything you like. Conflicts are flagged as you type.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What does Lilo not have that Arc has?</summary>
        <p>
          Sync, a Windows version and a mobile app. Lilo has no account and no
          server, so there is nothing to sync through. Your password manager
          keeps syncing your passwords everywhere.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Could Lilo shut down like Arc?</summary>
        <p>
          Not the way Arc does. Lilo is MIT licensed, so the app and its code
          stay yours to run and fork, and there is no server or account that
          could be switched off. The engine is the WebKit that ships with macOS,
          so Apple's security fixes keep arriving with macOS updates. Lilo is
          made by Alexays, who has also maintained the open-source Waybar since
          2018.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Is Lilo free?</summary>
        <p>
          Yes. Lilo is free and open source under the{" "}
          <a href="https://github.com/Alexays/Lilo/blob/main/LICENSE">
            MIT license
          </a>
          . There is no paid tier, no account and no telemetry.
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </PageSection>
</Layout>
